msgid "How do we measure startup time"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Some frameworks use aggressive lazy initialization techniques.\n"
"It is important to measure the time to first request (TTFR) to most "
"accurately reflect how long a framework needs to start.\n"
"Otherwise, you will miss the time the framework _actually_ takes to "
"initialize."
msgstr ""

msgid "Here is how we measure startup time in our tests."
msgstr ""

msgid "Approach"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We record a timestamp immediately before launching the application, start "
"polling the application's HTTP endpoint, launch, and then continue polling "
"until it returns a 200 response. The difference is the Time to First Request "
"(TTFR)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://github.com/quarkusio/spring-quarkus-perf-comparison/blob/main/"
"scripts/perf-lab/scripts/time-to-1st-request.sh[`time-to-1st-request.sh`] "
"script in the benchmark repository implements this precisely. It records the "
"start time using nanosecond granularity:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A background polling loop attempts TCP connections and sends HTTP GET "
"requests until a 200 response is received:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The TTFR is then computed as the difference between the response timestamp "
"and the start timestamp, in nanoseconds."
msgstr ""

msgid "Why not just use the framework&#8217;s reported startup time?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A framework-reported startup time (e.g. \"started in 0.002s\") only measures "
"internal initialization.\n"
"It will not include time spent loading shared libraries, JVM bootstrap, and "
"there may also be a further gap between the claimed ready-time and the time "
"when the application can actually serve a request.\n"
"Measuring TTFR end-to-end captures the full cost."
msgstr ""

msgid "How do we measure throughput"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Throughput is measured using https://hyperfoil.io[Hyperfoil], a tool "
"designed to avoid the <<coordinated-omission,coordinated omission problem>>.\n"
"We run Hyperfoil via https://www.jbang.dev[JBang], which handles downloading "
"and launching it automatically."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://github.com/quarkusio/spring-quarkus-perf-comparison/blob/main/"
"scripts/perf-lab/main.yml[performance lab] invokes Hyperfoil as follows:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://github.com/quarkusio/spring-quarkus-perf-comparison/blob/main/"
"scripts/perf-lab/load-tests/load-test-fixed-threads-read-all.hf."
"yml[Hyperfoil configuration] defines three phases:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Warmup** — 2 minutes of traffic with 100 concurrent connections "
"(configurable)"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Cooldown** — a 30-second pause"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Load test** — 30 seconds of measured traffic with 100 concurrent "
"connections"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The configuration uses these defaults, which can be overridden with `-P` "
"flags:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

msgid "Default"
msgstr ""

msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

msgid "`CONNECTIONS`"
msgstr ""

msgid "100"
msgstr ""

msgid "Number of concurrent connections"
msgstr ""

msgid "`THREADS`"
msgstr ""

msgid "2"
msgstr ""

msgid "Hyperfoil executor threads"
msgstr ""

msgid "`WARMUP_DURATION`"
msgstr ""

msgid "2m"
msgstr ""

msgid "Duration of warmup phase"
msgstr ""

msgid "`LOAD_DURATION`"
msgstr ""

msgid "30s"
msgstr ""

msgid "Duration of measured load test"
msgstr ""

msgid "`PATH`"
msgstr ""

msgid "/fruits"
msgstr ""

msgid "Endpoint under test"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Throughput is calculated from the Hyperfoil results as requests completed "
"divided by elapsed time."
msgstr ""

msgid "JVM flags for benchmarking"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The benchmark scripts apply these JVM flags when running the application "
"under test:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`-XX:ActiveProcessorCount` limits how many processors the JVM believes are "
"available, ensuring consistent thread pool sizing across environments.\n"
"`-Xms` and `-Xmx` are set to the same value to eliminate heap resizing "
"during the measurement."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the performance lab, CPU affinity is further controlled using `taskset` "
"to pin processes to specific cores, preventing interference between the "
"application, database, load generator, and monitoring."
msgstr ""

msgid "Additional flags applied by Quarkus"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When Quarkus invokes GraalVM `native-image` it will apply some additional "
"flags by default."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You might want to know about the following ones in case you're comparing "
"performance properties with other builds."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disable fallback images"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Fallback native images are a feature of GraalVM to \"fall back\" to run your "
"application in the normal JVM, should the compilation\n"
"to native code fail for some reason."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus disables this feature by setting `-H:FallbackThreshold=0`: this will "
"ensure you get a compilation failure rather\n"
"risking to not notice that the application is unable to really run in native "
"mode."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you instead want to just run in Java mode, that's totally possible: just "
"skip the native-image build and run it as a jar."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disable Isolates"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Isolates are a neat feature of GraalVM, but Quarkus isn't using them at this "
"stage."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disable via `-H:-SpawnIsolates`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disable auto-registration of all Service Loader implementations"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus extensions can automatically pick the right services they need, "
"while GraalVM's native-image defaults to include\n"
"all services it's able to find on the classpath."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We prefer listing services explicitly as it produces better optimised "
"binaries. Disable it as well by setting `-H:-UseServiceLoaderFeature`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Others &#8230;&#8203;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This section is provided as high level guidance, but can't presume to be "
"comprehensive as some flags are controlled\n"
" dynamically by the extensions, the platform you're building on, "
"configuration details, your code and possibly\n"
" a combination of these."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Generally speaking the ones listed here are those most likely to affect "
"performance metrics, but in the right\n"
"circumstances one could observe non-negligible impact from the other flags "
"too."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you're to investigate some differences in detail make sure to check what "
"Quarkus is invoking exactly: when the build\n"
"plugin is producing a native image, the full command lines are logged."
msgstr ""

msgid "Coordinated Omission Problem in Tools"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When measuring performance of a framework like Quarkus the latency "
"experience by users are especially interesting and for that there are many "
"different tools. Unfortunately, many fail to measure the latency correctly "
"and instead fall short and create the Coordinate Omission problem. Meaning "
"tools fails to accommodate for delays to submit new requests when system is "
"under load and aggregate these numbers making the latency and throughput "
"numbers very misleading."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A good walkthrough of the issue is https://www.youtube.com/watch?v="
"lJ8ydIuPFeU[this video] where Gil Tene the author of wrk2 explains the issue "
"and https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xdG8b9iDYbE[Quarkus Insights #22] have "
"John O'Hara from Quarkus performance team show how it can show up."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Although that video and related papers and articles date all back to 2015 "
"then even today you will find tools that fall short with the coordinated "
"omission problem."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tools that at current time of writing are known to exhibit the problem and "
"should NOT be used for measuring latency/throughput (they may be used for "
"other things):"
msgstr ""

msgid "JMeter"
msgstr ""

msgid "wrk"
msgstr ""

msgid "Tools that are known to not be affected:"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://github.com/giltene/wrk2[wrk2]"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://hyperfoil.io[Hyperfoil] — this is what the https://github.com/"
"quarkusio/spring-quarkus-perf-comparison[Quarkus benchmark suite] uses"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Mind you, the tools are not better than your own understanding of what they "
"measure thus even when using `wrk2` or `hyperfoil` make sure you understand "
"what the numbers mean."
msgstr ""
